1. Create a Context
First, you need to create a context using React.createContext(). This function returns a context object.
import React, { createContext, useState } from 'react';

const ThemeContext = createContext();

export default ThemeContext;

2. Create a Provider Component
Next, create a provider component that will hold the state and provide it to its children.
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import ThemeContext from './ThemeContext';

const ThemeProvider = ({ children }) => {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');


  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>
      {children}
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
};

export default ThemeProvider;

3. Use the Provider in Your Application
Wrap your application (or part of it) with the provider component to make the context available to the components within it.
i

 the Provider in Your Application
Wrap your application (or part of it) with the provider component to make the context available to the components within it.
import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';
import App from './App';
import ThemeProvider from './ThemeProvider';

ReactDOM.render(
  <ThemeProvider>
    <App />
  </ThemeProvider>,
  document.getElementById('root')
);

4. Consume the Context
You can consume the context in any component that needs the shared state. There are two ways to consume context: using the useContext hook in functional components and the Context.Consumer in class components or functional components.
Using useContext Hook (Functional Components)
