Access Modifiers in Java control who can access a class, method, variable, or constructor. They are one of the most commonly asked Java interview topics.
Types of Access Modifiers in Java
Java has 4 access modifiers:
1. private
A private member is accessible only within the same class.
Example
class Employee {

    private int salary = 50000;

    public void display() {
        System.out.println(salary);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Employee emp = new Employee();

        // ERROR
        // System.out.println(emp.salary);

        emp.display();
    }
}
Output
50000
Key Points
Most restrictive access modifier.
Used for encapsulation.
Cannot be inherited directly.
2. Default (Package-Private)
If you don't specify any access modifier, Java uses default access.
class Employee {

    int id = 101;     // default access
}
Only classes i

(Package-Private)
If you don't specify any access modifier, Java uses default access.
