2. Inner (Nested) Classes
If you define a class inside another class, it acts as a member of that outer class. In this specific case, you can use all four modifiers: [14]
private Inner Class: Only accessible inside the outer class. Excellent for hiding implementation details.
protected Inner Class: Accessible within the same package and by any subclasses of the outer class. [15, 16, 17, 18, 19]
Code Example:
public class OuterClass {

    // Valid: Only OuterClass can see and use this
    private class PrivateInner {
        void display() { System.out.println("Private Inner"); }
    }

    // Valid: Subclasses of OuterClass can see and use this
    protected class ProtectedInner {
        void display() { System.out.println("Protected Inner"); }
    }
}

