The most common modifiers you will use every day are public, private, and protected. The others (internal, etc.) become important when you start breaking a large application into multiple separate projects (assemblies).
The private protected modifier is one of the most specific security levels in C#. It means: "You can only access this if you are a child class AND you live in the exact same project (assembly)."
It is typically used when you are building a class library for other developers to use. You might have base variables you want your own internal child classes to share, but you absolutely don't want outside developers messing with them when they inherit from your classes in their own separate projects.
